# <%= module_name %>

A [GemStack](https://github.com/gemstack/gemstack) application: a Ruby API with a
Next.js + TypeScript frontend, served from one origin.

```bash
gemstack dev          # http://localhost:3000  (/api/* → Ruby, everything else → Next.js)
bin/gsk dev            # same command through this app's bundle
gemstack routes       # list API routes
gemstack test         # run the Ruby tests
gemstack console      # IRB with the app loaded
gemstack generate controller Status show
```

## Layout

| Path | What lives there |
|---|---|
| `config/app.rb` | configuration (`GemStack.configure`) |
| `config/routes.rb` | API routes, relative to `/api` |
| `app/` | autoloaded code: `controllers/`, and any folder you add (`services/`, ...) |
| `test/` | Minitest + rack-test (`GemStack::TestCase`) |
| `frontend/` | the Next.js app; `lib/gemstack/client.ts` talks to the API |

## Production

Run the API with `gemstack server` (Puma, `config/puma.rb`) and the frontend
with `npm run build && npm start` in `frontend/`. Either route `/api/*` to the
API with your reverse proxy, or set `GEMSTACK_API_URL` for the Next.js process
and it will proxy `/api/*` itself.
